</tr>
<tr><th class="News">08-16</th><td class="News"><a title="Mainstream Linux is an Arch-based Linux distribution with a highly customised desktop using the Quickshell toolkit on top of the Hyprland compositor. It includes a variety of user-friendly features, such a Material You desktop layout, per-workspace layout switching, a session restore option, a gaming mode, as well as a graphical settings tool to customise displays, themes, key bindings, networking and updates. The distribution uses the Calamares system installer with several install modes and applies automatic Btrfs snapshots before every update." href="mainstream">Mainstream OS</a></td></tr>
<tr><th class="News">08-15</th><td class="News"><a title="SkillFishOS is a gaming-focused Linux distribution based on Debian's &quot;Unstable&quot; branch and featuring a themed KDE Plasma desktop. Originally developed for the AMD BC250 compact motherboard built around AMD's Cyan Skillfish accelerated processing unit (APU), it is also available for generic 64-bit personals computers. SkillFishOS ships with Steam, EmuDeck, ES-DE, Heroic and Proton gaming clients pre-installed. It also includes a tailored Linux kernel, pre-configured Btrfs snapshots, a steampunk-themed dark desktop, Unsloth Studio application designed to run and export open-source artificial intelligence (AI) models, and a remote control option. Besides gaming, the distribution is also suitable for emulation work, local AI and general desktop use." href="skillfishos">SkillFishOS</a></td></tr>
<tr><th class="News">08-14</th><td class="News"><a title="Valtux OS is a desktop Linux distribution based on Debian's &quot;Stable&quot; branch and featuring the KDE Plasma desktop. Developed in Switzerland, it provides out-of-the-box support for all official languages (French, German, Italian and Romansh) and keyboard layouts used in the country. The distribution includes many popular productivity and multimedia software and is intended for all types of users - from complete Linux beginners to experienced power users." href="valtux">Valtux OS</a></td></tr>
<tr><th class="News">08-13</th><td class="News"><a title="ThorOS is a Debian-based desktop Linux distribution featuring the GNOME desktop. Its principal feature is &quot;voice control&quot;. Using artificial intelligence and a voice assistant called &quot;Jarvis&quot;, it is capable to execute commands to accomplish a great variety of tasks. Examples include &quot;Jarvis, open Firefox&quot;, &quot;Jarvis, write a thank-you note to my landlord&quot;, or &quot;Jarvis, what's 15% of 240&quot;. Jarvis is also able to create, open, read, write, move, rename, search and delete files, launch applications, answer general questions, remind the user about appointments, and run terminal commands. The distribution's artificial intelligence assistant is private by default, but can be configured to use a remote source of knowledge, if desired." href="thoros">ThorOS</a></td></tr>
<tr><th class="News">08-12</th><td class="News"><a title="Nebula Linux is a set of two distinct Linux distributions for the desktop based either on Debian's &quot;Stable&quot; branch or on Arch Linux, with the former featuring a vanilla KDE Plasma desktop, while the latter, a rolling-release variant, integrating the DankMaterialShell (DMS) desktop shell on top of the niri Wayland compositor. Some of the features of Nebula Linux include out-of-the-box support for Flatpak packages, a custom Nebula Welcome app, a branded Calamares system installer, a separate command-line installation option, and support for Btrfs snapshots and rollbacks integrated into the GRUB boot menu." href="nebula">Nebula Linux</a></td></tr>
<tr><th class="News">08-10</th><td class="News"><a title="Umbra Linux is a Linux distribution built entirely from scratch, using the Linux From Scratch (LFS) book. Its goal is to provide a lightweight, educational system that gives full control over the build and configuration process. The distribution uses the Xfce desktop, systemd init software, and an experimental package manager called moonpkg." href="umbra">Umbra Linux</a></td></tr>
<tr><th class="News">08-09</th><td class="News"><a title="Vuu-do is a minimalist desktop Linux distribution featuring primarily the Openbox window manager. Despite its small size, the distribution ships with several popular productivity applications, such as LibreOffice, GIMP, Inkscape, VLC and Transmission, as well as various custom utilities, including VuuNotes (a lightweight note-taking application), VuuMan (a graphical man-page browser and viewer), VuuPaper (a wallpaper manager). Vuu-do can be installed to a hard disk using the Refracta system installer. Besides the main branch, the project also builds a separate Devuan-based ISO image using the Xlibre X11 display server and the MATE desktop environment." href="vuudo">Vuu-do</a></td></tr>
<tr><th class="News">08-07</th><td class="News"><a title="CaramOS is a Vietnamese desktop Linux distribution based on Linux Mint and featuring the Cinnamon desktop. The project's focus is to make Linux easier for Vietnamese users switching from proprietary operating systems, such as a familiar desktop layout and out-of-the-box support for Vietnamese input. The distribution bundles various popular applications, including browsers (Chrome and Firefox), an office suite (WPS Office), and practical utilities for daily use." href="caramos">CaramOS</a></td></tr>
<tr><th class="News">08-05</th><td class="News"><a title="JRescue is a Debian-based live Linux distribution featuring a variety of tools to perform maintenance on a computer. It uses the lightweight LXQt desktop environment. Some of the utilities provided on the live image include boot-repair and disk-partitioning tools, disk usage analysers, a hardware information utility, Clonezilla and Rescuezilla, the ClamAV antivirus suite, the KDE ISO image writer, and the WoeUSB-ng program. JRescue also includes the Firefox web browser." href="jrescue">JRescue</a></td></tr>
<tr><th class="News">08-04</th><td class="News"><a title="starkOS is an independently-developed GNU/Linux distribution designed for experienced Linux users. It uses a ports system and the stark package manager to install and update software from source code. The project provides a lightweight live image with Sway Wayland compositor and some basic software on it. The live system does not include an installer; however, starkOS can be installed to a hard disk using one of the rootfs filesystems (with a choice of runit, s6 or SysV init software) provided separately. The project's goal is to develop a Linux distribution that is simple, lightweight, up-to-date and extensible." href="starkos">starkOS</a></td></tr>
